      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The realist view doesn't necessarily make international law a complete myth, but it suggests that its enforcement is highly contingent on power. Most modern historians agree that international law is more accurately a sophisticated set of norms and shared expectations than a globally binding police force. In reality, it often functions more as a \"performance\" to legitimize state actions, while powerful actors strategically choose when and how rigidly to adhere to its rules.",
      "source": "target",
